%S A397676 0,0,0,4,0,8,0,28,24,36,0,56,0,80,88,140,0,140,0,200,184,216,0,280,
%T A397676 160,308,312,428,0,384,0,620,472,540,464,664,0,680,664,908,0,800,0,
%U A397676 1124,1016,1008,0,1224,504,1276,1144,1592,0,1472,1104,1880,1432,1620,0,1704
%N A397676 Sum of absolute differences of consecutive terms of n/gcd(n,k), k = 1..n-1.
%C A397676 Equivalently, a(n) is the sum of the absolute differences of consecutive entries among the first n-1 entries of row n of A054531, whose k-th entry is n/gcd(n,k).
%C A397676 Since n/gcd(n,k) is the additive order of k in the additive cyclic group Z/nZ, a(n) is the sum of the absolute differences of the additive orders of consecutive nonzero residues 1..n-1.
%C A397676 For n > 1, a(n) = 0 if and only if n is prime. If n is prime, gcd(n,k) = 1 for every 1 <= k < n, so all n-1 terms are n. Conversely, if n is composite, some proper divisor k of n satisfies n/gcd(n,k) < n, whereas the first and last terms are both n, so the sum of the absolute differences is positive.
%C A397676 Let N_n(d,e) = #{k mod n : gcd(n,k)=d and gcd(n,k+1)=e}, where d and e divide n. Since gcd(k,k+1)=1, N_n(d,e)=0 whenever gcd(d,e)>1. For n > 1, a(n) = n*Sum_{d|n} Sum_{e|n} N_n(d,e)*abs(1/d - 1/e) - 2*(n-1).
%C A397676 The last term removes the two transitions involving residue 0 from the corresponding cyclic sum of absolute differences.
%H A397676 Tomas Laris, <a href="/A397676/b397676.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A397676 a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n-2} abs(n/gcd(n,k+1) - n/gcd(n,k)).
%F A397676 a(p^r) = 2*(p^(2*r) - p^(r+1) - p^r + p)/(p+1), p prime, r >= 2.
%F A397676 a(p*q) = 2*(p-1)*(p*q - p + q^2 - 3*q), p < q distinct primes.
%F A397676 a(2*p) = 2*p^2 - 2*p - 4, p prime.
%e A397676 For n = 6, the first n-1 terms n/gcd(n,k) are 6, 3, 2, 3, 6. Hence a(6) = |6-3| + |3-2| + |2-3| + |3-6| = 8.
%e A397676 For n = 5, the corresponding terms are 5, 5, 5, 5, so a(5) = 0.
%t A397676 A397676[n_] := 2*Total[Abs[Differences[n/GCD[n, Range[n/2]]]]];
%t A397676 Array[A397676, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 13 2026 *)
%o A397676 (Python)
%o A397676 from math import gcd
%o A397676 def a(n):
%o A397676     return sum(abs(n//gcd(n,k+1) - n//gcd(n,k)) for k in range(1, n-1))
%Y A397676 Cf. A054531 (the underlying array n/gcd(n,k)).
%Y A397676 Cf. A277227 (additive orders of the elements modulo n).
%Y A397676 Cf. A057660 (sum of row n of A054531).
%Y A397676 Cf. A058026 (number of k mod n such that gcd(k,n) = gcd(k+1,n) = 1).
%K A397676 nonn,new
%O A397676 1,4
